 "interpretation": {
  "screen": "The padded candidate (all W8A16 calls padded to 32 rows) measured below every control on every metric (about -9% versus the control median), but the three unpadded controls themselves spread about 10% in run order 39.3, 40.4, 36.8 tok/s c1 wall, with the last control only 2.7% above the candidate. This boot cannot resolve effects of that size; the sign is consistent across metrics but the magnitude is not trustworthy.",
  "mechanism_from_microbenchmark": "With a deeper warmup the per-call cost of padding is unambiguous: the K=3072 shapes (gdn_out_proj, attn_o_proj) roughly double from about 13 us at M=2 to about 24-27 us at 32 rows, mlp_down_proj rises 83 to 94-96 us, mlp_gate_up_proj 164 to 175-177 us, gdn_in_proj_qkvz 74 to 82 us, and attn_qkv_proj 65 to 70 us. Summed over 64 layers that is about 2-3 ms per decode step, matching a several-percent loss. The census lead itself was an artifact: the census timed each shape with one warmup call and ten iterations and reported attn_qkv_proj at 172-183 us for M<=16; with ten warmups and 250 iterations the same kernel measures 64.8 us at M=2, so oneDNN has lazy first-call work on that shape, not a slow steady-state kernel.",
  "decision": "R118 and the qkv-only R118b variant are closed. Fixed-row padding buys batch-shape invariance only at a measurable decode cost; it is not a performance lead. The census script now uses ten warmups and thirty iterations so this cannot recur.",
